---
import ReferenceTable from '../../components/ReferenceTable.astro';
import Button from '../../components/Button.astro';
import ContentCard from '../../components/ContentCard.astro';
import DocumentLayout from '../../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl, seo } from '../../data/seo';

const meta = seo.implement;
const siteUrl = Astro.site?.toString() || 'https://tensor-standards.pages.dev/';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: meta.title,
  description: meta.description,
  url: absoluteUrl(siteUrl, meta.canonicalPath),
};
---

<DocumentLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} canonicalPath={meta.canonicalPath} ogType={meta.ogType} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <ContentCard>
<p class="section-kicker">Core 0.1.0-draft.1 · Getting started</p>
    <h2>Implementation quick start</h2>
    <p>For an introduction to the model, <a href="/start/">TENSOR in five minutes</a> follows a SOC investigation before the technical workflow below.</p>
    <p>The reference implementation validates portable investigation bundles. A bundle contains an investigation record and the exact definitions referenced by that record. The browser and command-line tools use the same candidate binding.</p>
    <div class="cta-group"><Button variant="primary" href="/investigation-lab/?case=soc-signin">Open the SOC case in the Lab</Button><Button variant="secondary" href="/extensions/">Vendor and organization graphs</Button></div>

    <h2>Browser workflow</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open the <a href="/investigation-lab/?case=soc-signin">SOC sign-in case in the Investigation Lab</a> and follow its walkthrough.</li>
      <li>Inspect Questions, Evidence, Assessments, Decisions, and History. The advanced Entry replay view shows individual retained entries and selected transitions.</li>
      <li>Download the bundle and import that JSON file to inspect the retained record and validation result.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Local files are processed in the browser without upload. The <a href="/core/0.1.0-draft.1/examples/case-a.bundle.json" download>agent-transfer bundle</a> and <a href="/core/0.1.0-draft.1/examples/case-b.bundle.json" download>identity bundle</a> are also available directly.</p>

    <h2>Command-line workflow</h2>
    <p>The reproducible Core baseline uses Node.js 24.21.0, npm 11.19.0, and the source commit pinned in the <a href="/review/">technical review package</a>:</p>
    <pre><code>{`git clone https://github.com/tensor-standards-consortium/TENSOR-Framework.git
cd TENSOR-Framework
git checkout --detach c82cd1c540b2ca6fbf5ba7ee3e38edcd2e1e6f8f
npm ci
npm run check
node core/v0.1.0-draft.1/cli.mjs core/v0.1.0-draft.1/examples/case-a.bundle.json`}</code></pre>
    <p>The CLI returns a report with <code>pass</code>, <code>fail</code>, or <code>indeterminate</code> results and individual checks. <a href="/conformance/">Validation</a> explains those results and the information needed in an implementation report.</p>

    <h2>Integration roles</h2>
    <ReferenceTable label="Integration roles"><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Implementation work</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Definition producer</td><td>Publish versioned questions, parameters, evidence criteria, and transitions with exact-byte digest pins.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Record producer</td><td>Record question instances, evidence references, attributed assessments, decisions, and revisions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reader</td><td>Display supported records and identify missing or unsupported interpretation dependencies.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exchanger</td><td>Import and export records, exact definition artifacts, and retained optional data; report any losses.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transition interpreter</td><td>Resolve explicitly selected assessments and transitions against their pinned definitions.</td></tr>
    </tbody></ReferenceTable>
    <p>An implementation can support one or several roles. Its report identifies the binding version, supported profiles and extensions, checks performed, and exchange limitations.</p>

    <h2>Vendor and organization integration</h2>
    <p>Vendor graphs connect public questions to product-specific evidence, additional checks, and integrations. Organization graphs add local policy, escalation, ownership, and workflow. The <a href="/extensions/">layering guide</a> includes a working graph-layer example and the separate Core extension format.</p>

    <h2>Artifacts and exchange</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/read/core/0.1.0-draft.1/binding/">Binding rules</a> define object identity, parameters, references, revisions, and extensions.</li>
      <li><a href="/schemas/">JSON Schemas</a> define structure; the semantic validator additionally checks references, pins, and transition selections.</li>
      <li>Evidence references can point to controlled stores. The receiving implementation manages evidence access and action authorization.</li>
      <li>Cross-tool exchange compares question context, evidence links, assessments, decisions, revisions, and declared losses.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href="/contribute/">Implementation reports</a> provide a public place for reproducible results. The supplied cases are illustrative; a local round trip establishes reference-tool behavior rather than independent interoperability.</p>
  </ContentCard>
</DocumentLayout>
